= Computer network =

== Overview ==
A '''computer network''' is a collection of interconnected computing devices that exchange data using communication links and protocols.

Networks range from local device connections to global internetworks.

== Nodes and links ==
A network contains endpoints and intermediate devices connected through wired, wireless, optical, or virtual links.

Links differ in bandwidth, latency, reliability, topology, and transmission medium.

== Protocols ==
A network protocol defines rules and message formats for communication.

Protocols coordinate issues such as addressing, framing, routing, reliability, congestion control, naming, and application semantics.

== Layering ==
Networking is commonly organized into layers.

A simplified Internet-oriented stack includes:

* link;
* network;
* transport;
* application.

Layering separates responsibilities but real implementations can cross boundaries for performance or control.

== Internet ==
The Internet is a global internetwork using the Internet Protocol family.

It is an important computer network, but:

<pre>
Internet ≠ computer network in general
</pre>

Private networks, local networks, sensor networks, data-center fabrics, and specialized networks also exist.

== Network and distributed system ==
A network provides communication.

A [[Distributed system]] adds coordinated application computation, replicated or partitioned state, failure handling, and system-level behavior across networked components.

== Related concepts ==
[[Distributed system]] and [[System boundary]] distinguish communication substrate from the larger coordinated systems built on top of networks.

